  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nWednesday turned out to be more active than anticipated, especially \nacross the Rio Grande Plains. Similar conditions are in store for \ntoday with a mid level trough across S TX combined with deep \nmoisture. Several models keep the highest probabilities across the \nVictoria Crossroads with very low chances across the Rio Grande \nPlains. This is due to the mid level trough shifting slightly \nnortheastward with a strengthening mid/upper level northerly flow \ndeveloping across the western areas and leading to a more subsident \nenvironment. That being said, the deep moisture and unstable \nenvironment may be enough to overcome the subsidence.  \n\nRainfall totals through Friday are expected to be generally less \nthan 0.25 inch, but with PWATs continuing to range from 2.0-2.3 \ninches, a few stronger storms may produce 1-2 inches. Rain chances \nare progged to decrease through Friday afternoon and into the \nweekend as mid level ridging builds back across S TX. \n\nAn Extreme risk of heat related impacts are forecast across most of \nS TX by the weekend. Although, heat indices up to 113 may occur this \nafternoon , it is expected to be isolated with a short duration, \nthus a Heat Advisory will not be issued at this time. However, by \nFriday and into the weekend the duration of heat indices of 110+ \nacross a larger area may warrant daily Heat Advisories, possibly \nthrough early next week.",
